CHANGES SINCE VERSION 0.931

-- 2000.09.23 ET:

1. Madeline now assigns single letters A-Z (excepting the letter E which
   is the constant base of the natural logarithms) as scratch numeric
   variables.  These are normally only useful 

2. Madeline now supports the following additional assignment operators
   for numeric values:
   
   += -= *= /=

3. Madeline now also supports the following postfix increment 
   and decrement assignment operators:
   
   ++ --

   These can only be used as postfix operators.  Prefix operation is
   not supported.
   
4. Madeline now supports "?" as an operator substitute for the "show"
   or "what is" command.  Here are some examples of what you can do 
   with these new features:
   
   M>? 12+3
   15
   M>? WebViewer
   "netscape"
   M>? PostscriptViewer
   "ghostview"
   M> ?EvaluationInterval
      0.5
   M> EvaluationInterval+=0.5
   M> ?EvaluationInterval
      1
   M> ?OffEndDistance
      10
   M> OffEndDistance--
   M> ?OffEndDistance
      9

   In previous versions, you would have had to type the command "show", 
   e.g., "show OffEndDistance", so the question mark is a little time
   saver.

   Programmers will recognize the convenience of the expanded
   set of assignment operators, including the increment and decrement
   operators.  To increase the value of a numeric variable in previous
   versions, you would have had to do something like this:

   M> OffEndDistance=OffEndDistance+0.5
   
   ... which is clearly more laborious.


